n  is really about to invade  could the collapse of the soviet union  teach us anything about russia's  intentions this week's magazine we have  a review of the long song of tchaikovsky  street written by peter waterdrinker  which is a first-hand account of the  soviet union's final years  professor mark galiotti is an expert in  modern russian history and he joins me  now alongside lsc's professor vladislav  ziebach author of collapse the fall of  the soviet union  mark vladislav thank you for joining me  this week  mark we have a review in the magazine of  peter waterdrinker's book the long song  of tchaikovsky street which follows the  end of the soviet union  what did you make of it  well i mean i really enjoyed it i think  i enjoyed it for several reasons  one is just simply it actually spoke to  my own experiences this was just the  same sort of time that i was just  beginning my academic career  i did my phd with a lot of in on country  research in the last three years of the  declining decaying collapsing soviet  union  so you know a lot of it in admittedly  much much less exciting way than peter  water drinkers experiences but a lot of  it you know spoke to the stuff that i  remembered but secondly i i very much  liked the way that it connected the  collapse of the soviet union with the  collapse of czarist russia and the  revolutions of 1917 in sometimes a  fairly savage way i wouldn't necessarily  agree with  the history of all of it  but the passionate way it interconnected  these notions of collapses revolutions  and countries devouring themselves  and yet still potentially bringing forth  hope that i thought was was very nicely  encapsulated by the book  so water drinker was in the soviet union  in the 1980s can you tell us a little  bit about what that experience might  have been like  oh boy well i mean the very very first  time i ever went to the soviet union was  actually in  1980 um time of the moscow olympics when  moscow was trying to sort of sp